E2 fibre form: k=#distinct p-free parts, m=max fibre size. Claim X: G>=k*m. Claim Y (all that the two branches give): G>=max(k,p^(m-1)).
 p 2 n 3 first counterexample to X (set,G,k,m): ((1, 2, 3), 3, 2, 2)
 p 2 n 4 first counterexample to X (set,G,k,m): ((1, 2, 3, 4), 4, 2, 3)
 p 2 n 5 first counterexample to X (set,G,k,m): ((1, 2, 3, 4, 5), 5, 3, 3)
 p 2 n 6 first counterexample to X (set,G,k,m): ((1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6), 6, 3, 3)
 p 3 n 3 first counterexample to X (set,G,k,m): ((1, 2, 3), 3, 2, 2)
 p 3 n 4 first counterexample to X (set,G,k,m): ((1, 2, 3, 4), 4, 3, 2)
 p 3 n 5 first counterexample to X (set,G,k,m): ((1, 2, 3, 4, 5), 5, 4, 2)
 p 3 n 6 first counterexample to X (set,G,k,m): ((1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6), 6, 4, 2)
E3 lattice (Ahlswede-Daykin) form: #distinct gcd(a,L/b) >= n, L=lcm(A); vs #distinct a/gcd(a,b)
 n 3 min #gcd(a,L/b) = 3
 n 4 min #gcd(a,L/b) = 4
 n 5 min #gcd(a,L/b) = 5
 n 6 min #gcd(a,L/b) = 6
 n 7 min #gcd(a,L/b) = 7
 S= (2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18) quotients [1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9] gcd(a,L/b) [1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18]
E4 CRT analogue for n=pq: T(p,q)=max size of a set of numbers p^e q^f with all quotients < pq
 p=2 q=3 n=6: phi(n)=2, fibre containing 1 can have 4 elements [1, 2, 3, 4]; counting bound n <= 2*4=8 (need < 6)
 p=2 q=5 n=10: phi(n)=4, fibre containing 1 can have 5 elements [1, 2, 4, 5, 8]; counting bound n <= 4*5=20 (need < 10)
 p=3 q=5 n=15: phi(n)=8, fibre containing 1 can have 4 elements [1, 3, 5, 9]; counting bound n <= 8*4=32 (need < 15)
 p=2 q=7 n=14: phi(n)=6, fibre containing 1 can have 5 elements [1, 2, 4, 7, 8]; counting bound n <= 6*5=30 (need < 14)
 p=3 q=7 n=21: phi(n)=12, fibre containing 1 can have 4 elements [1, 3, 7, 9]; counting bound n <= 12*4=48 (need < 21)
 p=5 q=7 n=35: phi(n)=24, fibre containing 1 can have 4 elements [1, 5, 7, 25]; counting bound n <= 24*4=96 (need < 35)
 explicit n=6: A= [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, 15, 20] classes mod 6 of the {2,3}-free part: 1 and 5, fibres {1,2,3,4} x {1,5}; within-fibre quotients: 4 G(A)= 20
